row produced from one original record describes that same record, so each
should keep that record's true \verb{has_been_*} values regardless of which
flag triggered its own creation: an "Admitted->ED" escalation splits one
row (\code{HasBeenE = 1}, \code{HasBeenAdmitted = 1}) into two synthetic rows (\code{ED}
and \code{Admitted}), and both must still show \code{has_been_e = 1} and
\code{has_been_admitted = 1}. Without this, \verb{has_been_*} end up \code{NA} (or,
worse, silently wrong after cross-pull \code{max()} reconciliation) for any row
that never came from \code{inpatient_admission_data} directly. \code{HasBeenI},
when excluded to prefer \code{HasBeenAdmitted}, is deliberately left out of the
pivot rather than dropped from the data, so its true 0/1 value survives on
every row.

